Before I run the search, a couple of preferences to confirm:
- Chicago airport: OK to use ORD (O’Hare), or do you prefer MDW?
- Tokyo: OK with either Haneda (HND) or Narita (NRT), or HND only?
- Shanghai: OK with Pudong (PVG) and/or Hongqiao (SHA)?
- Any airline preferences or time windows?

What “economy class” usually means on these routes (quick guide):
- Fare types
  - Basic Economy: usually no changes or upgrades, limited or paid seat selection, lower mileage earning; checked bag may not be included on some US carriers.
  - Standard/Main Economy: changes allowed for a fee or fare difference, seat selection included/cheaper, better mileage earning.
- Baggage (varies by airline/fare)
  - Long-haul US–Japan: often 1–2 checked bags at 23 kg each; ANA/JAL typically allow 2; US carriers can be 1 on Basic, sometimes 1–2 on Main.
  - Tokyo–Shanghai: many fares include 1 checked bag (23 kg); some include 2. Carry-on plus personal item allowed, with Asian carriers sometimes enforcing 7–10 kg carry-on limits.
- Onboard
  - Seat pitch around 31–33 inches; Premium Economy (optional upgrade) is ~38 inches and much more comfortable for the long transpacific.
  - Seatback entertainment, power/USB, full meal service with snacks; alcohol policies vary (ANA/JAL typically include, US carriers often include wine/beer on international).
- Tips
  - If comfort matters, consider Premium Economy for the ORD–TYO and PVG–ORD legs.
  - Try to keep all segments on one airline/alliance to harmonize baggage rules.
  - Choose seats early (front/mid cabin for quicker immigration on arrival).

Entry/visa notes to verify:
- Japan: many nationalities are visa-free for short stays—confirm for your passports.
- China: most travelers need a visa for a ~10-day stay; the 144‑hour visa‑free transit doesn’t cover 10 days. Check requirements for your citizenships well in advance.

If 2025 is correct and you’re fine with ORD and both Tokyo (HND/NRT) and Shanghai (PVG/SHA) airports, I’ll search economy for 2 adults and show you the best/cheapest/fastest options.

